# 2026-08-19 Locked out of Hetzner server, no SSH access

## What I was trying to do
SSH into my Hetzner server so that I could restart a docker container.
## What broke
I wasn't able to get in. My SSH key had a passphrase that I never wrote down, and I also didn't know my root password.
## What I thought was wrong
I thought I just forgot the password and that I would be able to reset it through the rescue mode on Hetzner.
## What was actually wrong
There were two things that made rescue mode hard. The Hetzner console uses a Finnish keyboard, so typing in a password with capital letters or symbols ended up coming out wrong each time. Also, rescue mode makes a new password each time you enable it again, so when I tried to login before rebooting the server the password did not work. I hit these at the same time and didn't know which one was the problem. 
## How I found it
I found out the Finnish keyboard when I saw that what I typed didn't work after doing the reboot right. I saw the layout was different when I saw what it listed on output. 
## How I fixed it
I made a new SSH key on my Mac without a passphrase
```
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f ~/.ssh/id_rescue -N ""
```
I put the public key on Hetzner's SSH Keys page. And I enabled rescue mode again and rebooted so that the new key would load into the system. I then SSH'd in
```
ssh-keygen -R 89.167.37.101
ssh -i ~/.ssh/id_rescue root@89.167.37.101
```
When I got into rescue, I set a new root password
```
mount /dev/sda1 /mnt
chroot /mnt passwd
```
Then I used the 'reset root password' button on Hetzner to reset it and then I rebooted to the normal server. I logged into Hetzner with the new password and got in. 
## Takeaway
2 things to do
- Dont set an SSH passphrase or write it down
- Only use lowercase letters and numbers in passwords because the Finnish layout might mess it up
